/P 119 0 R
/T (Chasing is exhausting. It narrows the horizon to a single point - the thing taken, the thing withheld, the thing owed. It convinces us that wholeness lies elsewhere. It fixes our gaze on the hand that removed, rather than the hand that still creates.)
/E (Chasing is exhausting. It narrows the horizon to a single point - the thing taken, the thing withheld, the thing owed. It convinces us that wholeness lies elsewhere. It fixes our gaze on the hand that removed, rather than the hand that still creates.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
